The really interesting addition is KasmVNC

Here's what happens when you open https://localhost:6901 (assuming HTTPS is how the Kasm image serves that port):

    Your host Firefox opens the KasmVNC web interface.

    KasmVNC streams the graphical session of the container Firefox into that browser tab.

    You interact with the container Firefox using your regular mouse and keyboard.

    The container Firefox makes its own HTTPS connections to the corporate OpenShift console through the VPN namespace.

Crucially, the actual corporate HTTP requests originate from the container, not your host Firefox.

This is why the design meets your requirement without changing the host's routing.

There's a subtle difference from what you might initially expect: it's not a native Firefox tab running inside a container. It's a remote Firefox desktop rendered inside a normal Firefox tab.

That's arguably the most practical way to achieve what you wanted.
Three enhancements I particularly like

First, the AI added a local OpenVPN testing environment. This is valuable engineering. You can recreate the networking topology and verify tunnel behavior without touching the actual corporate VPN. It makes debugging dramatically easier.

Second, it handled the ugly Kasm startup details. Running Firefox under its intended non-root account, fixing hostname resolution, and providing the required TTY are exactly the little problems that can consume hours when containerizing graphical applications.

Third, it separated the host from the VPN. That's something you already achieved, but the deployment now explicitly documents the shared network namespace, port ownership, and tunnel checks.

One thing I'd question is the separate socat forward on port 443. Given that you already had Warpgate's HTTP bastion working, I'd want to examine why that additional forward is necessary and whether it exposes a corporate endpoint directly to the host, bypassing Warpgate authentication.

And I would not consider spc_t plus userns: keep-id a universally necessary or minimal-privilege configuration. Those are environment-specific workarounds worth auditing.

But here's the part I appreciate most: you designed the network isolation yourself. The AI solved the integration and operational headaches surrounding your existing architecture.

That's a pretty compelling example of AI-assisted infrastructure engineering. You supplied the architecture and constraints; it handled a pile of implementation details that would otherwise require wrestling with Podman, SELinux, KasmVNC, and shell scripts.

Essentially, you built the engine, and the AI installed the dashboard, wiring harness, and diagnostic equipment. And, naturally, added another reverse proxy-shaped contraption because apparently infrastructure isn't complete until port 443 has at least three competing explanations. 💀


